\section{The density}{

The parameters are \code{mu}, \code{sigma} and \code{quantile}, as in
\code{asym_laplace()}. A fixed smoothing \code{lambda} (the argument
\code{smoothing}) is added. Let \code{e = y - mu}, \code{p = quantile},
\code{a = lambda (1 - p)} and \verb{b = lambda p}. Then the log density is

\verb{(1 - p) e / sigma - lambda log(1 + exp(e / (lambda sigma))) - log(lambda sigma) - log B(a, b)}.

This is the ELF density of Fasiolo et al. (2021) with their
\code{lambda}. It is also the density of qgam's \code{elf()} family, whose
argument \code{co} is \verb{lambda sigma}. The response is
\verb{y = mu + lambda sigma logit(Z)} with \code{Z ~ Beta(a, b)}. So:
\itemize{
\item the distribution function is the incomplete beta
\verb{pbeta(plogis(e / (lambda sigma)), a, b)};
\item the mean is \verb{mu + lambda sigma (digamma(a) - digamma(b))};
\item the variance is \verb{(lambda sigma)^2 (trigamma(a) + trigamma(b))}.
}

As \code{lambda} goes to 0, the density goes to the asymmetric Laplace
density of \code{asym_laplace()} with the same \code{mu}, \code{sigma} and
\code{quantile}.

Hold \code{quantile} at the quantile you want, as in
\code{bf(y ~ x, quantile = 0.9)}. If you do not, \code{quantile} is estimated,
as in \code{asym_laplace()}. Then \code{mu} is no longer a quantile of
interest.
}

\section{What mu estimates}{

At its maximum, the likelihood sets the mean of
\verb{plogis(e / (lambda sigma))} to \code{1 - p}. So \code{mu} is the \code{p} quantile
of \verb{y - lambda sigma L}, where \code{L} is a standard logistic variable.
This is the quantile of the response smoothed by a logistic kernel
of scale \verb{lambda sigma}. It is not the quantile of \code{y} itself.

The difference is 0 for a residual density that is symmetric about
the quantile, such as the median of a symmetric law. It grows with
the square of \code{lambda}. It does not shrink as the sample grows.

Let \code{f} be the residual density and \code{q} the quantile. For a kernel
width that is small against the scale on which \code{f} changes, the
difference is about \verb{-(pi^2 / 6) (lambda sigma)^2 f'(q) / f(q)}.
This is an approximation only. At \code{smoothing = 1} it can be off by a
factor of 2. Near an end of the support it can have the wrong sign.

The table gives the bias of \code{mu} as \code{n} goes to infinity. The units
are the residual sd, and the standard error of the quantile estimate
at \code{n = 1000}. It was computed by numerical integration
(\code{dev/squant-pop.R} in the source repository), not from the
approximation.

