\section{Choosing the smoothing}{

\code{smoothing} is a fixed constant of the family, like \code{k} of \code{\link[=huber]{huber()}}.
It is not estimated. Maximum likelihood would choose the \code{lambda}
that fits the shape of the residuals. A large \code{lambda} moves \code{mu}
away from the quantile.

qgam chooses the absolute width \verb{lambda sigma} from a rule that
balances the bias against the variance of the estimate (its argument
\code{err}). With this rule, the width shrinks as \code{n^(-1/3)}. qgam then
chooses \code{sigma}, its "learning rate", to calibrate its posterior
credible intervals. It does not use the likelihood for either. Here
\code{sigma} is a parameter of the density, estimated by maximum
likelihood, and \code{lambda} is relative to it. The default is fixed. It
does not change with the data. Use these steps:
\enumerate{
\item Keep the default \code{0.25} for a model with fixed effects only and a
quantile between 0.1 and 0.9.
\item Use a smaller value, such as \code{0.1}, for a large sample. There a
bias of a fixed size is a larger part of the standard error.
\item Use a larger value, such as \code{1}, for a model with a group-level
term in \code{mu} (see "Group-level terms"). Accept a larger bias away
from the median.
\item To see the effect of the choice, fit two values and compare \code{mu}.
}
}

\section{Standard errors}{

The default \code{summary()}, \code{fixef()}, \code{confint()} and \code{hypothesis()}
show the Hessian standard errors of the ELF model. The residuals of
a quantile regression do not usually follow the ELF density. Then
these standard errors are not those of the quantile estimate. Away
from the median, their 95\% intervals cover only about 0.7 to 0.8
(table below). \code{\link[=frm_bootstrap]{frm_bootstrap()}} draws from the fitted ELF, so it has
the same problem.

Use the sandwich covariance \code{\link[=vcov_cluster]{vcov_cluster()}} with one cluster per
row. It is the quantile regression's own covariance, with a kernel
estimate of the residual density. Do these steps:
\enumerate{
\item Add a column that numbers the rows, \code{dd$row <- seq_len(nrow(dd))},
before the fit.
\item Make the covariance: \code{V <- vcov_cluster(fit, ~ row, "CR0", full = TRUE)}.
\item Give it to the inference methods: \code{summary(fit, vcov = V)} and
\code{confint(fit, vcov = V)}.
}

\code{vcov(fit, cluster = ~ row)} gives the coefficient block of the
sandwich, with the default small-sample correction of
\code{\link[=vcov_cluster]{vcov_cluster()}}.

The table gives the ratio of the mean standard error to the Monte
Carlo sd of the slope. It also gives the coverage of 95\% Wald
intervals for the true quantile slope. The settings are
\code{smoothing = 0.25}, 400 rows, and 300 data sets per row
(\code{dev/squant-se.R}). The bootstrap had 200 draws on 3 data sets of
the second row.

