\section{Group-level terms}{

With \code{(1 | g)} in \code{mu}, the Laplace approximation falls as the group
sd grows without end, so it has a highest point. That point can be
the boundary \code{sd = 0}. For \code{asym_laplace()} it rises without end
instead. The test
designs had 80 rows in groups of 2 or 4, or 100 rows in groups of 10,
with 10 data sets each. On them, every fit stopped at code 0 with no
warning.

But at a small smoothing the approximation is poor. Its profile in
the group sd is a sawtooth with several local maxima. The cause is
the log determinant of the inner Hessian, which jumps as a group's
mode crosses a data point. So a fit can stop on a local maximum, with
code 0 and no warning.

Compared with \code{quadrature = TRUE} at the median, the log group sd
differed by up to 0.87 at \code{smoothing = 0.1}, 0.59 at \code{0.25}, 0.31 at
\code{0.5} and 0.18 at \code{1}. There were 40 such fits and 10 at the 0.9
quantile. Of these, at least 7, 2, 1 and 0 stopped below a higher
point of a 10-point profile (\code{dev/squant-re.R}). On one data set at
the 0.9 quantile, a finer profile was 3.42 higher than the fit at
\code{smoothing = 0.1}, and 1.76 higher at \code{0.25}. At the 0.9 quantile
with groups of 4, the log sd differed from quadrature by 0.28 even at
\code{smoothing = 1}. Quadrature fitted all 200 of these models.

An exact \code{gp()} term is slow at a small smoothing. At the default
\code{0.25}, \code{y ~ gp(x) + x2} warned that the Laplace approximation is
not reliable, and took 129 s at 40 rows and 501 s at 80. At
\code{smoothing = 1} it took 0.6 s and 3.1 s. The time is in the inner
Newton search (\code{dev/reviews/squant-short-gp.R}).

Use these steps for a model with a group-level term:
\enumerate{
\item Use \code{smoothing = 1}.
\item For the estimates, use \code{quadrature = TRUE} where it applies (one
scalar grouping factor).
\item For standard errors, know what each route gives:
\itemize{
\item The Laplace fit: \code{vcov_cluster(fit, ~ g, "CR0", full = TRUE)}
is the cluster sandwich, robust to the ELF's misspecification.
Its coverage was not measured for this family.
\item The quadrature fit: \code{\link[=vcov_cluster]{vcov_cluster()}} refuses it, so only the
Hessian standard errors of the ELF model are available. They are
not calibrated for the quantile estimate.
}
}
}

\section{How to choose between asym_laplace() and quantile_elf()}{

Both families estimate a quantile. Use the steps below to choose.
\enumerate{
\item If the model has a group-level term (\code{(1 | g)}, \code{s()}, \code{gp()})
in \code{mu}, and you fit it with the Laplace approximation, use
\code{quantile_elf()}. \code{asym_laplace()} has no maximum there, and the
fit is refused.
\item If you need standard errors or \code{\link[=confint]{confint()}} from the fit itself,
use \code{quantile_elf()} with the sandwich covariance:
\code{confint(fit, vcov = vcov_cluster(fit, ~ row, "CR0", full = TRUE))}
(see "Standard errors"). \code{asym_laplace()} has no Hessian standard
error.
\item If you need the exact sample quantile regression estimate, as
\code{quantreg::rq()} gives it, and only fixed effects, use
\code{asym_laplace()}. Or use \code{quantile_elf()} with a small \code{smoothing}.
\item If you will sample the model with frmtmb.sample's \code{frm_sample()}
to match a brms model, use \code{asym_laplace()}. brms has no ELF
family.
}
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
n <- 300
dd <- data.frame(x = runif(n), row = seq_len(n))
dd$y <- 1 + 2 * dd$x + (0.5 + dd$x) * rnorm(n)
fit <- frm(bf(y ~ x, quantile = 0.9), family = quantile_elf(),
           data = dd)

# the default summary shows the ELF model's Hessian standard errors
summary(fit)

# the sandwich standard errors of the quantile estimate
V <- vcov_cluster(fit, ~ row, "CR0", full = TRUE)
summary(fit, vcov = V)
confint(fit, vcov = V)
sqrt(diag(vcov(fit, cluster = ~ row)))
}
